<!--BlockML Documentation — code explanation rule

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.rule.CodeExplanationRule-->
<brule:CodeExplanationRule xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:brule="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.rule"
  xmlns:rule="org.blockml.bml.docgen.rule">

  <baseType>
    rule:ContentRule
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A content rule requiring code examples to be followed by a concrete explanation
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Defines how code examples must be integrated into the explanatory flow of a documentation
    section.
    
    A code example must not stand on its own. After every code block, provide explanatory text
    that connects the example back to the concept introduced by the surrounding section. The
    explanation must make explicit how the concrete elements shown in the example represent the
    concepts being taught.
    
    Refer to the actual names and constructs used in the example whenever this improves
    understanding. For example, identify which concrete Block is the definition, which nested
    element is an instance, which Property or Aggregation demonstrates the discussed behavior,
    or which expression illustrates the relevant language feature.
    
    The explanation should interpret the example rather than merely repeat what is visibly
    present in the code. Explain why the relevant elements matter, how they relate to each other,
    and how they demonstrate the point made before the example.
    
    Keep the explanation focused on the purpose of the example. It does not need to describe
    every line of code unless those details are necessary for understanding the concept.
  </documentation>
  <properties>
    <name override="true">
      <value>Code explanation</value>
    </name>
    <summary override="true">
      <value>Follow every code example with explanatory text that connects its concrete elements
to the concept being taught.</value>
    </summary>
    <instructions override="true">
      <value>After every code block, provide explanatory text that relates the example directly
to the concept introduced in the surrounding section. Refer to the concrete names,
elements, properties, aggregations, instances, expressions, or other constructs used in
the example whenever they help make the relationship explicit. Explain what role those
concrete elements play and why they demonstrate the concept. Do not merely paraphrase
the code or repeat the introductory text. Interpret the example and connect it back to
the section's explanation. Focus on the parts relevant to the example's teaching goal;
do not explain every line unless necessary.</value>
    </instructions>
  </properties>
</brule:CodeExplanationRule>
